A||||5||Lisa 86 2m4||din radio 1/2 equalizer?||||5||
Z||000000||Lisa 86 2m4||03-03-2001||09:33 PM||||A while back I asked about putting in a raido and Oreif said you could get creative and use a 1 din radio and 1/2 din equalizer  Well, I've been looking at a pioneer package that has a 1 din cassetter, 8 disc cd changer, if I use an equalizer to fill up the rest of the space, what else do I need? What else should I know? would i still need faceplates and adaptors? This is all greek to me! Thanks!!!!<BR>Lisa||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||03-03-2001||11:57 PM||||Some of the adaptor kits are made specifically to acomodate this. The one I have on my shelf is American International #GM-K444. I think it was $15 at K-mart. That's just the mount kit. no harness adaptors.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Lisa 86 2m4||03-04-2001||09:48 AM||||So, I do need an installation kit. Ok, now what about wire harness adaptors? And do I also need this antenna adaptor? This just keeps getting bigger and bigger! Sheesh!<BR>Maybe someone could give me an outline of everything I would need?<BR>Thank you!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-04-2001||10:09 AM||||OK, First if you go with any aftermarket radio you can buy a harness adapter. But if you want to use a 1/2 din equalizer you either need an amplified one or you will need an external amplifier. If you buy an amplified Equalizer and since the EQ will sit right under (or above, your choice) the radio it can be spliced into the adapter harness. If you buy an passive (non-amplified) EQ and wish to add a 4-channel amp, you won't be using the factory speaker wires. The EQ has to be between the radio and the amp/speakers.<BR>You will need an adapter to mount the radio and EQ. There are many kits available. The kit is required so that the radio and EQ are supported correctly and spaced evenly. (there is a smalll gap between them for mounting purposes. From the front you won't see the gap since the overhang covers it)<BR>The antenna adapter depends on the radio used. Fiero's use the larger antenna plugs and a lot of newer cars use the smaller. You may need the adapter. They are inexpensive. I think I've seen them at Radio Shack for $5.00.<P>When doing Car Stereo systems, every time you add something it can get bigger faster.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MoneypitGT||03-04-2001||08:28 PM||||You won't need the ant. adaptor. Another kit is a Metra 994544, and the harness is a 701677-1 or DW1677-1, same thing. That will give you standard colors to match up with the Pioneer stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MontanaFiero||03-05-2001||01:30 AM||||Lisa,<BR>Here's another suggestion... Both Pioneer and Jensen make a "din and a half" size deck that fits right in the original hole, doesn't stick out and (I believe) mounts without a kit. The Jensen is available with <I>both</I> a cassette and a cd player in the same unit. JVC makes a "double din" unit (also with cd/tape) that can be "made to fit" by trimming the plastic instrument panel cover where the radio and heater controls are (a sacrifice). I also believe these have some sort of graphic equalizer and fader built in, and even the Jensen sounds awesome when used with an external amplifier.<BR>Check Best Buy and Sears and look at the latest Crutchfield catalog.<P>Have fun   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;<p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 03-05-2001).]||||reg||3
